erson run `worldlabs auth login` in their own\nterminal and enter the key at its private prompt. Never ask for a key in chat or put one in a\ncommand. Automation can set `WLT_API_KEY`. The key selects the API project and its billing. A\npassing status check confirms asset access, not every task's permission.\n\nThe CLI needs network access. In a sandbox that blocks it, ask for approval instead of retrying.\n\n## Run a task\n\n```sh\nworldlabs tasks list                                        # task names\nworldlabs assets upload photo.jpg --json                    # note the \"id\"\nworldlabs tasks run <task> --request request.json --wait --json > op.json\nworldlabs operations download <operation-id> --out results\n```\n\n- **Request.** A request names an uploaded file as `{\"assetId\": \"<id>\"}`. The workflow skills\n  hold the requests for their own tasks (`images2PosedRGBD`, `atlasGenerate`,\n  `posedImages2Splats`). For any other task, the fields are on its guide page, linked from\n  https://docs.atlas.worldlabs.ai/task-overview.md; read that page before writing the request.\n  `https://docs.atlas.worldlabs.ai/llms.txt` lists every page.\n- **Wait.** `--wait` stops waiting after 10 minutes unless `--timeout <seconds>` says otherwise.\n  The task keeps running. Resume with `worldlabs operations wait <operation-id> --json`.\n- **Retry.** `tasks run` prints an idempotency key. If a submission is interrupted, run it again\n  with the same request and `--idempotency-key <key>` so the task is not started twice.\n- **Result.** A finished operation has `response` or `error`; check `error` first.\n  `operations download` saves every output file to a new directory, named for its place in the\n  response (`frames_003_image.jpg`, `frames_003_depth.exr`), and writes `operation.json` with\n  each file's local path. If the command is missing, update the CLI.\n- **Errors.** The CLI does not print the API's reason. HTTP 422 means the request body was\n  rejected: check it against the task's guide page. HTTP 402 means the project is out of credits\n  or over its spend limit. HTTP 401 or 403 is the key or its permissions. An operation that ends\n  `THROTTLED` hit a capacity limit, not a problem with the request: tell the person, wait a few\n  minutes, and run it again. See https://docs.atlas.worldlabs.ai/errors.md\n\n....\nworldlabs-cli/SKILL.md:3:description: Run World Labs Atlas API tasks from a terminal with the worldlabs CLI. Covers install and sign-in, uploading files, running a task from a JSON request, waiting for it, and downloading its outputs. Use for any Atlas job without a skill of its own, such as generating or editing an image, estimating camera and depth, or extracting a mesh, and for CLI setup and errors. New views of a scene belong to atlas-splats or atlas-flythrough. Also covers the connected World Labs tools when there is no shell.\nworldlabs-cli/SKILL.md:56:  rejected: check it against the task's guide page. HTTP 402 means the project is out of credits\nworldlabs-cli/SKILL.md:61:Tasks spend the project's credits. Run the tasks the request needs without quoting prices or\natlas-splats/SKILL.md:3:description: Turn one or more photos or a generated image into a 3D scene as Gaussian splats (an SPZ file) with World Labs Atlas. Use when the user wants a 3D scene, a world to explore, splats, or a scene to view, export or mesh from images, or new angles and views of a place from an image.\natlas-splats/SKILL.md:68:   The result is an SPZ file named for its splat count (`refinedSplats_2000000.spz`), in the\natlas-splats/SKILL.md:89:- For a mesh, pass the SPZ asset to `splats2Mesh` as `splatAsset`.\n# Screen 05 — World Labs Atlas pilot plan (NOT RUN — awaiting approval)\n\n> **ATLAS ACTIVE — 2026-11-15 ~22:20 UTC (account 6):** after the user re-signed in, `list_projects` (free) answers:\n> project **\"My project\"** (`proj_ad4fdb076f4a4c80934406cfefc4d30b`, workspace \"Personal workspace\"),\n> `credits_available: true`, task too